# Coin Change over the canonical LeetCode fixture: coins `[1 2 5]` and amount
# 11, whose answer is 3 coins (5 + 5 + 1). The unbounded-knapsack dynamic
# program: `dp[v]` is the fewest coins summing to `v`, relaxed upward once per
# coin, so each coin may be used any number of times. `require` pins
# `dp[11] == 3`, so a `Valid` outcome IS the proof that 3 is optimal for this
# input: the table was filled by the recurrence and the verifier followed every
# relaxation.
#
# 99 stands in for "no way to make this amount". It only has to exceed the
# largest real answer (11, all ones), and a candidate built from it is 100,
# which never wins a comparison against a real entry. Making the sentinel a
# plain number rather than a maximum keeps the arithmetic in range.
#
# The inner loop starts at `v = coin`, so `v - coin` is never negative and the
# byte offset into `dp` never wraps.
#
# Registers: a0 = the amount, a1 = element size (4), a2 = the coin count,
#            a3 = the sentinel then the answer, a4 = the coin index,
#            a5 = the coin's value, a6 = v, t0 = an address,
#            t1/t2 = byte offsets, t3 = the candidate, t4 = dp[v].

coins: [u32]*3 = [1, 2, 5]
dp: [u32]*12

a0 = 11
a1 = 4
a2 = 3
a3 = 99

# --- dp[0] = 0, every other amount starts unreachable ---
t0 = &dp
t1 = 0
t0[0] = t1
t2 = 1
while t2 <= a0:
    t1 = t2 * a1
    t0 = &dp
    t0 = t0 + t1
    t0[0] = a3
    t2 = t2 + 1

# --- relax the table once per coin, upward so a coin can repeat ---
a4 = 0
while a4 < a2:
    t1 = a4 * a1
    t0 = &coins
    t0 = t0 + t1
    a5 = t0[0]
    a6 = a5
    while a6 <= a0:
        t1 = a6 - a5
        t2 = t1 * a1
        t0 = &dp
        t0 = t0 + t2
        t3 = t0[0]
        t3 = t3 + 1
        t2 = a6 * a1
        t0 = &dp
        t0 = t0 + t2
        t4 = t0[0]
        if t3 < t4:
            t0[0] = t3
        a6 = a6 + 1
    a4 = a4 + 1

# --- the proof: 11 takes exactly 3 coins from [1 2 5] ---
t1 = a0 * a1
t0 = &dp
t0 = t0 + t1
a3 = t0[0]
t0 = 3
require a3 == t0

# `print` clobbers a0-a2/a7/t0-t2/t5; a3 survives it.
print(a3)
print("\n")
exit(0)
